# Built SPA. dist/ is the //go:embed target, so two files must stay committed:
# //go:embed requires the path to exist at compile time, and index.html plus the
# bundle marker are what make `GOWORK=off go build ./...` and
# `GOWORK=off go test ./...` work with no Node toolchain installed --
# bundle.go's BundleProtocolVersion reads looprig-bundle.json out of the
# embedded tree, so a build with no marker is a build that cannot answer.
# `npm run build -w app` overwrites everything under dist/, marker included;
# `make release-dist` force-adds the result onto a release commit.
dist/*
!dist/index.html
!dist/looprig-bundle.json

# npm workspaces
node_modules/
packages/*/dist/
packages/*/node_modules/
app/dist/
app/node_modules/

# Binaries
/bin/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Go build artifacts
*.test
*.out

# Environment & secrets
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key

# Editor
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Git worktrees
.worktrees/

# Claude Code project settings
.claude/

# This module does not vendor. go.mod pins exact versions and go.sum verifies
# their content hashes; a vendor tree would add only offline builds and
# source-level dependency diffs, at the cost of masking which version a
# GOWORK=off build actually resolves. Ignored so a stray `go mod vendor` cannot
# be committed by accident.
/vendor/
